pmc:NewThoughtShinnDiseaseWillReturn a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Relapse through continued destructive thinking"@en, "Rückfall durch fortgesetztes zerstörerisches Denken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Instant healing is possible by realizing the body is a perfect idea held in the divine mind, but disease returns if hoarding, hatred, fear or condemnation continue (go and sin no more); the soul must be washed clean for permanent healing."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a failure condition: the text names an outcome that goes wrong or does not come, and its cause. It names how pmc:NewThoughtShinnBodyRenewed fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Intuition or Guidance, p. 70; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnCondemnation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Condemnation attracts the condemned thing"@en, "Verurteilung zieht das Verurteilte an"@de ;
    skos:definition "Condemning others draws disease, unhappiness and the very act condemned. Case: a deserted wife who kept condemning the other woman later fell for a married man herself; criticism is like seizing a live wire."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a failure condition: the text names an outcome that goes wrong or does not come, and its cause. It names how pmc:NewThoughtShinnBodyRenewed fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
