pmc:NewThoughtShinnBadReceivers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Refusing gifts blocks one's channels"@en, "Geschenke ablehnen blockiert die Kanäle"@de ;
    skos:definition "Refusing gifts out of pride blocks one's channels and leads to having little. Case: a generous woman who refused several thousand dollars soon owed exactly that sum."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a failure condition: the text names an outcome that goes wrong or does not come, and its cause. It names how pmc:NewThoughtShinnPlenty fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Perfect Self-Expression or the Divine Design, p. 82; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnBalanceOfGivingAndReceiving a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Balance of giving and receiving"@en, "Gleichgewicht von Geben und Nehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Giving and receiving balance; one gives without thought of return but violates law by not accepting returns, since all gifts are from God through human channels."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Perfect Self-Expression or the Divine Design, p. 82; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnThoughtOfLack a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Never think lack over a giver"@en, "Nie Mangel über den Gebenden denken"@de ;
    skos:definition "One must not pity a giver as unable to afford it; the author saw the one-cent giver as rich, which she says brought his supply. A bad receiver must learn to accept even a postage stamp."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NewThoughtShinnTithing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Perfect Self-Expression or the Divine Design, p. 83; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnReincarnation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reincarnation explains unequal births"@en, "Wiedergeburt erklärt ungleiche Geburten"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is no chance; man passes through many births until he knows the freeing truth, drawn back by unsatisfied desire, karmic debt or destiny. Those born rich and healthy held such pictures in a past life; one manifests the sum of subconscious beliefs."@en ;
    skos:note "Author's term: \"law of reincarnation\"."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Perfect Self-Expression or the Divine Design, p. 83; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnBirthlessAndDeathless a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The real man is birthless and deathless"@en, "Der wahre Mensch ist ungeboren und unsterblich"@de ;
    skos:definition "Birth and death are laws made by man, stemming from the Adamic fall into belief in two powers; the spiritual man was never born and never dies. Truth frees one from karma, sin and death, and freedom comes through fulfilling the Divine Design."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Perfect Self-Expression or the Divine Design, p. 84; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .


#
# Denials and Affirmations
#

pmc:NewThoughtShinnAccomplishedFact a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "All good already accomplished in divine mind"@en, "Alles Gute im göttlichen Geist schon vollbracht"@de ;
    skos:definition "All good to be manifested already exists in the divine mind and is set free by recognition or the spoken word; one must therefore decree only the Divine Idea, since idle words decree failure (Job 22:28)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 85; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmp:NewThoughtShinnPerfectWay a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Demanding under grace and perfectly"@en, "Unter Gnade auf vollkommene Weise fordern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Demanding from Infinite Spirit the right home, friend, position or sum that is one's own by divine right, and giving thanks that it now comes under grace and in a perfect way; the closing clause is called the most important part, and as financial consciousness grows one demands large sums in the same form."@en ;
    skos:note "Author's term: \"divine selection\"; \"under grace\"."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 85; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing", "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 86; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnNotInAPerfectWay a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Demand money in a perfect way"@en, "Geld auf vollkommene Weise fordern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Case: a woman who asked for one thousand dollars received it as indemnity for her daughter's injury; demands must specify receipt under grace and in a perfect manner."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NewThoughtShinnPerfectWay ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 85; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnLimitedExpectancies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Limited expectancy limits receipt"@en, "Begrenzte Erwartung begrenzt den Empfang"@de ;
    skos:definition "One cannot release more than one thinks possible; expectancies must be enlarged. Case: a student who demanded six hundred dollars got exactly that though nearly a thousand had been coming."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a failure condition: the text names an outcome that goes wrong or does not come, and its cause. It names how pmc:NewThoughtShinnPlenty fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 86; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnGoldNugget a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Consciousness of opulence as the gold nugget"@en, "Bewusstsein der Fülle als Goldklumpen"@de ;
    skos:definition "After a French legend of a brass nugget believed to be gold, riches depend on consciousness: feeling rich brings riches."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 87; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmp:NewThoughtShinnJourneysEnd a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Beginning at the journey's end"@en, "Am Ziel der Reise beginnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In demanding, declaring one has already received; affirming continually to establish belief in the subconscious, giving thanks repeatedly rather than pleading."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 87; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnPerfectFaithOnce a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "With perfect faith one affirmation suffices"@en, "Bei vollkommenem Glauben genügt eine Affirmation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Repetition is needed only because faith is imperfect; with perfect faith a single affirmation would do. One ought not to beg or implore."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 87; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnCommandAndDemand a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Prayer as command, demand and praise"@en, "Gebet als Befehl, Forderung und Lobpreis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Lord's Prayer is read as command and demand ending in praise; rejoicing while still in the desert opens the way. The student's work is to make himself believe all is possible with God."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 87; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnRealizationIsManifestation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Realization is manifestation"@en, "Verwirklichung im Bewusstsein ist Manifestation"@de ;
    skos:definition "To demonstrate one must reach an inner realization, which the author equates with manifestation."@en ;
    skos:note "Author's term: \"realization\"."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 88; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnPinkPapercutter a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Papercutter as bridge of active faith"@en, "Brieföffner als Brücke tätigen Glaubens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Case: needing a large sum, a woman asked for a lead, felt drawn to buy a fine papercutter fit for opening large cheques, saw herself doing so, and received the money within weeks."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NewThoughtShinnDefiniteLead, pmp:NewThoughtShinnDiggingDitches ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NewThoughtShinnSumAtTheRightTime ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 88; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnThoughtOfOxygen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Subconscious directed in faith"@en, "Im Glauben gelenktes Unterbewusstes"@de ;
    skos:definition "Story: a man who smashed a bookcase pane, believing it a window, slept well, having supplied himself with oxygen by thought; offered as one of many stories of the subconscious directed in faith."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 88; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnNeverTurnBack a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Never turn back or compromise"@en, "Nie umkehren oder Kompromisse eingehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once begun, one must not waver, give up or settle for less (James 1:7); a student's vow to accept nothing less than asked, only more, is praised. Standing firm is often the hardest phase."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NewThoughtShinnJourneysEnd ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 89; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnTemptationToCompromise a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Temptation to give up or compromise"@en, "Versuchung aufzugeben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The temptation to quit, turn back or compromise comes in the hardest phase of demonstrating; the waverer receives nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a failure condition: the text names an outcome that goes wrong or does not come, and its cause. It names how pmp:NewThoughtShinnJourneysEnd fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 89; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnEleventhHour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Demonstration at the eleventh hour"@en, "Demonstration in letzter Stunde"@de ;
    skos:definition "Announced: demonstrations often arrive at the last moment because one then lets go and stops reasoning, giving Infinite Intelligence room to work."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success announced in advance: the text tells the student to expect it before the outcome."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 89; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnImpatientDesires a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Impatient desires fulfilled violently"@en, "Ungeduldige Wünsche gewaltsam erfüllt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Joyless desires get joyless answers and impatient ones are delayed or violently fulfilled. Case: a woman who kept wishing her glasses gone kept breaking or losing them; she should have demanded perfect eyesight."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a failure condition: the text names an outcome that goes wrong or does not come, and its cause. It names how pmc:NewThoughtShinnEveryDesireADemand fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 89; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnDepreciationAndFearOfLoss a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Depreciation and fear of loss"@en, "Geringschätzung und Verlustangst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author names two attitudes of mind as the causes of loss: depreciation, as with the wife who did not value her husband, and fear of loss, which pictures the loss in the subconscious."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a failure condition: the text names an outcome that goes wrong or does not come, and its cause. It names how pmc:NewThoughtShinnPlenty fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 89; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnLettingGoYieldsInstantManifestation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Letting go yields instantaneous manifestation"@en, "Loslassen ergibt sofortige Manifestation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once the student can release his problem, that is, cast his burden, he is said to have instantaneous manifestation; the case of the umbrella mended a moment after it was put in God's charge illustrates it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NewThoughtShinnCastingTheBurden ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NewThoughtShinnWordMadeFlesh ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Game of Life and How to Play It (Marina del Rey, CA: DeVorss & Company, n.d.; copyright 1925, ISBN 0-87516-257-6), Denials and Affirmations, p. 90; read in the proofread transcription Project Gutenberg eBook #74878 (2024), made from the archive.org scan gameoflifehowtop00shin of this printing" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnUmbrellaMender a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Handing over brings the umbrella mender"@en, "Übergabe bringt den Schirmflicker"@de ;
    skos:definition "Case: a woman with a wrecked borrowed umbrella gave it to God and at once an umbrella mender offered his services; there is always such help when a situation is put in God's hands."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NewThoughtShinnGodsHands ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NewThoughtShinnFallsAwayOfItsOwnWeight ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnGameOfLife ;
